On Windows, WGL_EXT_swap_control is named in the GL extension list as well as in the WGL
one. Programs look for it there: SDL 1.2 (under the Quake III engine games, OpenArena among
them) looks nowhere else, as the graphics vendors' drivers name it in both lists. A program
that does not find it never asks for a swap interval, so it could not turn off the default
interval of 1: OpenArena's timedemo ran at the display's 60 frames a second, and at 340 with
the name in the list.

--- a/src/mesa/main/extensions.c
+++ b/src/mesa/main/extensions.c
@@ -169,6 +169,12 @@
    memset(&_mesa_extension_override_enables, 0, sizeof(struct gl_extensions));
    memset(&_mesa_extension_override_disables, 0, sizeof(struct gl_extensions));
 
+#ifdef _WIN32
+   /* Windows programs look for the swap interval extension in this list too (SDL 1.2 looks
+    * nowhere else), and the graphics vendors' drivers name it here. */
+   unrecognized_extensions.names[unknown_ext++] = "WGL_EXT_swap_control";
+#endif
+
    if (override == NULL || override[0] == '\0') {
       return;
    }
